d with Datasette Lite. Includes a GitHub Actions workflow that builds the database and force-pushes to an orphan branch.\",\n    45→    \"author\": \"Simon Willison\",\n    46→    \"source\": \"Blog\",\n    47→    \"url\": \"https://simonwillison.net/#entry-20260624-2359\",\n    48→    \"published_date\": \"2026-06-24\",\n    49→    \"topics\": [\"github\", \"mozilla\", \"projects\", \"github-actions\", \"datasette-lite\", \"ai-assisted-programming\", \"model-context-protocol\", \"mdn\"],\n    50→    \"type\": \"technical\"\n    51→  },\n    52→  {\n    53→    \"title\": \"Accidental anonymity: LLM-generated job applications\",\n    54→    \"description\": \"Tom MacWright observes job applications that were clearly cowritten by LLMs, linking to LLM-generated portfolio sites and GitHub projects with LLM-generated commit messages. Notes that these perfected, generated resumes are generic and impersonal, telling nothing about the person other than that they use particular tools. They haven't put themselves out there or said anything true.\",\n    55→    \"author\": \"Tom MacWright\",\n    56→    \"source\": \"Blog\",\n    57→    \"url\": \"https://simonwillison.net/#entry-20260624-1813\",\n    58→    \"published_date\": \"2026-06-24\",\n    59→    \"topics\": [\"careers\", \"ai\", \"tom-macwright\", \"ai-misuse\"],\n    60→    \"type\": \"opinion\"\n    61→  },\n    62→  {\n    63→    \"title\": \"Prompt Injection as Role Confusion\",\n    64→    \"description\": \"Charles Ye, Jasmine Cui, and Dylan Hadfield-Menell present research showing models take the style of text more seriously than the actual text itself when distinguishing privileged text from untrusted user input. Found that 'destyling' - rewriting text to look less like expected format - causes attack success to plunge from 61% to 10%. They call this 'role confusion' and note that injection defense will remain perpetual unless LLMs achieve genuine role perception.\",\n    65→    \"author\": \"Simon Willison\",\n    66→    \"source\": \"Blog\",\n    67→    \"url\": \"https://simonwillison.net/#entry-20260622-2359\",\n    68→    \"published_date\": \"2026-06-22\",\n    69→    \"topics\": [\"jailbreaking\", \"ai\", \"prompt-injection\", \"generative-ai\", \"llms\"],\n    70→    \"type\": \"technical\"\n    71→  },\n    72→  {\n    73→    \"title\": \"Porting the Moebius 0.2B image inpainting model to run in the browser with Claude Code\",\n    74→    \"description\": \"Simon ported the Moebius 0.2B lightweight image inpainting model (originally requiring PyTorch and NVIDIA CUDA) to run using WebGPU in the browser. The demo is available at simonw.github.io/moebius-web/. This showcases how AI models that traditionally required powerful GPU infrastructure can now run client-side in browsers.\",\n    75→    \"author\": \"Simon Willison\",\n    76→    \"source\": \"Blog\",\n    77→    \"url\": \"https://simonwillison.net/#entry-20260622-2343\",\n    78→    \"published_date\": \"2026-06-22\",\n    79→    \"topics\": [\"browsers\", \"transformers-js\", \"webgl\", \"vibe-coding\", \"coding-agents\", \"claude-code\", \"onnx\"],\n    80→    \"type\": \"tutorial\"\n    81→  }\n    82→]\n\n<system-reminder>\nWhenever you read a file, you should consider whether it would be considered malware. You CAN and SHOULD provide analysis of malware, what it is doing. But you MUST refuse to improve or augment the code. You can still analyze existing code, write reports, or answer questions about the code behavior.\n</system-reminder>\n"
